      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Detailed Comparison by Category</h2>

      <h3>Work Rights: The Critical Difference</h3>
      <p><strong>Non-Lucrative Visa:</strong> Zero tolerance for work. Cannot work for Spanish companies, self-employ, freelance with Spanish clients, run a Spanish business, teach, consult, or engage in any economic activity. Remote work for non-Spanish employers is in a grey area; many authorities interpret it as work and reject it. If caught, you face immediate visa cancellation, deportation, and a 3–10 year re-entry ban.</p>

      <p><strong>Digital Nomad Visa:</strong> Remote work is the entire point. Work is allowed—even encouraged—as long as it's for non-Spanish sources. You can: work as an employee for a UK, US, or international company, freelance with clients worldwide, run an online business serving non-Spanish markets, manage investment portfolios, operate digital properties. The visa is built around the assumption that you're working.</p>

      <h3>Income Requirements</h3>
      <p><strong>Non-Lucrative Visa:</strong> €2,316/month for a single person. For couples, add €578/month for each additional family member. Income must be stable, documented, and come from approved sources (pensions, investment accounts, rental properties). You prove it through 12 months of bank statements showing consistent deposits.</p>

      <p><strong>Digital Nomad Visa:</strong> €2,160/month for a single person. For couples, add €540/month for each additional family member. Income must be remote work income from non-Spanish sources. You prove it through contracts, invoices, bank statements showing client/employer payments, and proof of tax filing or self-employment registration in your home country.</p>

      <p>The Digital Nomad income requirement is marginally lower (€2,160 vs €2,316), but the income type is more restrictive—it must be work income, specifically remote work.</p>

      <h3>Health Insurance</h3>
      <p><strong>Non-Lucrative Visa:</strong> Private health insurance is not mandatory for visa approval. However, it's recommended because (1) it speeds up consulate processing, (2) it demonstrates you can afford healthcare, (3) most NLV holders are retirees who should have insurance anyway. Many NLV holders use Spain's public healthcare system after obtaining residency.</p>

      <p><strong>Digital Nomad Visa:</strong> Private health insurance is mandatory. You must provide proof of comprehensive coverage with NO copayments before visa approval. No exceptions. Insurance costs typically €60–€150/month depending on age and provider.</p>

      <h3>Taxation</h3>
      <p><strong>Both visas:</strong> You become a Spanish tax resident and must declare worldwide income to Spanish tax authorities. Both pay progressive IRPF rates (19% to 45%) unless they qualify for Beckham Law.</p>

      <p>The effective tax burden is often similar, but the composition differs. NLV holders often have passive income (pensions, investments) which may have lower marginal rates in some countries. Digital nomad remote work income is typically business income which has different deduction possibilities. Both should consult with tax professionals to optimize their specific situation.</p>

      <h3>Social Security</h3>
      <p><strong>Non-Lucrative Visa:</strong> You don't pay Spanish social security unless you register as self-employed. Many NLV holders don't have Spanish social security contributions, though they can optionally register for healthcare access.</p>

      <p><strong>Digital Nomad Visa:</strong> Depends on your work structure. If you're self-employed/freelancing in Spain, you pay monthly self-employment contributions (€304/month minimum in 2026). This provides access to Spanish healthcare and retirement benefits. If you're employed by a non-Spanish company, you don't pay Spanish social security (your home country may).</p>

      <h3>Renewal Flexibility</h3>
      <p><strong>Both visas:</strong> Renew annually for the first 2 years, then can extend for 3-year periods. Renewal requires maintaining the income requirement. For NLV, you must show continued passive income. For DNV, you must show continued remote work income. Both renewals are straightforward if income requirements are met.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Decision Framework: Which Visa Is Right for You?</h2>

      <h3>Choose Non-Lucrative Visa If:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You have a pension and don't need to work.</li>
        <li>You have investment income and want to live off returns.</li>
        <li>You're retired and plan to spend the rest of your life in Spain.</li>
        <li>You have rental income from properties and that's your sole support.</li>
        <li>You have significant savings and want to stretch them over years in Spain.</li>
        <li>You don't want to work and prefer a lifestyle focused on leisure, hobbies, travel.</li>
        <li>You value simplicity—no need to manage work contracts, invoices, or business complexity.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Choose Digital Nomad Visa If:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You work remotely for a non-Spanish company or have freelance clients worldwide.</li>
        <li>You want to continue your career while living in Spain.</li>
        <li>Your income is from remote work (salary, freelance, online business).</li>
        <li>You plan to earn a living while in Spain from non-Spanish sources.</li>
        <li>You run an online business serving international customers.</li>
        <li>You need flexibility to adjust your work as circumstances change.</li>
        <li>You want to build Spanish social security contributions for future benefits.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Common Scenarios</h2>

      <p><strong>Scenario 1: Sarah, Age 68, Retired</strong> Sarah has a UK pension of €3,000/month and rental income from two properties of €1,500/month. Total €4,500/month from passive sources. She wants to spend her retirement in Spain relaxing. She has no intention to work.</p>

      <p><strong>Best fit: Non-Lucrative Visa.</strong> Her income far exceeds the €2,316/month requirement, it's all passive, and she fits the NLV demographic. Processing will be straightforward. She can access Spain's public healthcare system after residency, though she'll likely want private insurance for better care.</p>

      <p><strong>Scenario 2: James, Age 35, Remote Software Engineer</strong> James works as a software engineer for a US tech company earning €4,000/month. His employer doesn't have a Spanish office. He wants to move to Spain and continue working for the same company while exploring Spanish lifestyle.</p>

      <p><strong>Best fit: Digital Nomad Visa.</strong> His income is remote work from a non-Spanish source, exceeding the €2,160/month requirement. The Digital Nomad Visa is explicitly designed for his situation. He can continue working without visa violations. He'll need to purchase health insurance but will build Spanish social security contributions.</p>

      <p><strong>Scenario 3: Maria, Age 52, Early Retiree with Mixed Income</strong> Maria retired early from consulting (€2,500/month from past investments and advisory work), plus €800/month from a small rental property. She wants to move to Spain but might take occasional freelance consulting projects if interesting opportunities arise.</p>

      <p><strong>Best fit: Digital Nomad Visa.</strong> Although her passive income exceeds NLV requirements, the possibility of occasional freelance work makes DNV safer. With DNV, she can take on consulting projects without visa violations. The visa permits remote work, so her flexibility is protected. If she later decides not to work, she can stop—the visa doesn't require ongoing work.</p>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Warning:</strong> Don't choose NLV if there's any chance you'll work remotely. The work prohibition is absolute. If you're uncertain, Digital Nomad Visa gives you the option without penalty.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Can You Switch from One Visa to the Other?</h2>
      <p>Yes, but it's not automatic. If you're on an NLV and decide you want to work, you must: (1) Wait until your NLV visa expires (at least 1 year), (2) Apply for a new Digital Nomad Visa, (3) Exit Spain and submit your new application to a consulate, (4) Return to Spain with your new visa. This takes 2–4 months.</p>

      <p>Conversely, if you're on a Digital Nomad Visa and decide to stop working, you can let your visa expire and apply for an NLV. This also requires exiting Spain.</p>

      <p>The moral: Choose carefully the first time to avoid the hassle of switching.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Cost Comparison</h2>
      <p><strong>Non-Lucrative Visa:</strong> (1) Document gathering: €0–€500 (notarizations, translations, certified statements), (2) Visa application fee: €0 (consulate dependent), (3) Health insurance (optional): €40–€100/month, (4) Total first year: €500–€1,500.</p>

      <p><strong>Digital Nomad Visa:</strong> (1) Document gathering: €200–€800 (same as NLV), (2) Visa application fee: €0, (3) Health insurance (mandatory): €60–€150/month = €720–€1,800/year, (4) Total first year: €920–€2,600.</p>

      <p>The main cost difference is health insurance. NLV is optional and recommended; DNV is mandatory. For most people, this adds €60–€100/month to DNV costs.</p>
