            <p>The core difference: NLV forbids work entirely; Digital Nomad allows remote work for non-Spanish employers. NLV requires passive income (pensions, investments); DNV requires remote work income. NLV costs less upfront to qualify; DNV has lower income requirements but allows work. Choose based on whether you want to work remotely in Spain.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What are the income requirements for each visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Non-Lucrative Visa: €27,792/year (€2,316/month) for a single person; increases for family members. Digital Nomad Visa: €2,160/month (€25,920/year) net income from remote work. DNV requires less income per year (€25,920 vs €27,792), but NLV doesn't care what type of passive income you have as long as you document it.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can you work on the Non-Lucrative Visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>No. The NLV explicitly prohibits all work—employment, self-employment, freelancing, even remote work for non-Spanish clients. Any work on an NLV is violation and can result in visa cancellation, deportation, and a 3–10 year re-entry ban. If you need to work, the Digital Nomad Visa is the correct choice.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can you work on the Digital Nomad Visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, specifically remote work. You can work for non-Spanish employers, freelance with international clients, run an online business with non-Spanish clients, or work for your own foreign company. You cannot work for Spanish employers or have Spanish clients/income sources. The visa is designed for remote workers whose income comes from outside Spain.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Which visa has lower taxes?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Both qualify for Beckham Law (flat 24% tax for 6 years on Spanish-sourced income). However, DNV digital nomads often have non-Spanish sourced income which typically has lower effective tax rates than passive income. NLV holders with investment income in Spain pay progressive IRPF rates. For remote workers, DNV often results in lower overall tax burden due to income source structure.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Which visa is easier to renew?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Both renew annually for the first 2 years, then can extend for longer periods. NLV renewal: prove you still have the required income, no major changes needed. DNV renewal: prove you still have remote work income, same monthly amount. Both are straightforward if you maintain the income requirement. Neither is significantly easier; renewal ease is similar.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
